{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RE-TYPED bug -> story and left OPEN and UNCLAIMED for a dedicated implementer. Not abandoned: the plan in the description is the current, four-times-reviewed design and is the right starting point.\n\nWHY. This began as a follow-up split out of silvern-dewy-damselfly's plan review — 'make the closed-without-signature state detectable by a later reader'. Four plan reviews grew it well past bug-follow-up size, and each round was legitimate discovery rather than wording:\n\nR1 BLOCK — a single boolean cannot express the multi-way discriminator the ACs demand, and the plan never said how 'a signature was expected' is computed or threaded (it is NOT verified_result, which is None for a --force close even though a signature WAS expected).\nR2 BLOCK — the enum omitted the DISPOSITION close, a genuinely distinct expectation that _completion_precheck already produces (close_precheck.py:441-446) and that would otherwise misread as a verified completion; and it omitted the config-unreadable fail-open, where recording gate_off would launder a FAULT into a policy choice. Also: AC2 requires reading from reduced state, so the reducer fold and ticket_state schema had to join the edit set.\nR3 BLOCK (new scope, which is why this stops here) — two structural findings the earlier rounds had not reached. gate_enabled (gates.py:79-87) returns a bare bool that is False for BOTH gate-off and config-unreadable, so it COLLAPSES the two before _completion_precheck can observe the difference: the enum cannot be produced without changing that function's signature, a new edit site the plan does not own. And idea->closed never enters _completion_precheck at all (close_ticket guards the call with current_status != 'idea'), so not_applicable has no named computation site.\n\nWHERE IT STANDS. The current description carries: an 8-value completion_expectation enum recorded on the close STATUS event in the same locked write as the close (no extra lock — which is the whole point, since an audit comment or second event would append through the very lock whose timeout causes the failure being recorded); a derivation table combining the durable expectation with the durable attestation; and the close_class precedent for event-write + fold-at-both-*->closed-edges + reduced-state schema.\n\nWHAT THE NEXT IMPLEMENTER MUST RESOLVE FIRST (the R3 findings):\n1. gate_enabled must distinguish gate-off from config-unreadable, or the expectation must be computed somewhere that can see the difference. Its current fail-open bool is the blocker.\n2. Name an owner for not_applicable, since idea->closed bypasses _completion_precheck entirely.\n3. Add ACs exercising gate_unreadable and disposition — R3 correctly noted both carry distinct semantics with no criterion asserting them.\n\nNOT BLOCKING ANYTHING SHIPPED. silvern-dewy-damselfly (change 1673) makes the loss machine-readable to the caller that suffered it across library, CLI and MCP, and is independent of this. This ticket is only about an unrelated LATER reader.\n\nRelated: defiant-orthoclase-buck (055d-f176-8fcb-4ffe) touches the same STATUS event with a much smaller change and should be sequenced with this, not merged into it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786566782500251001,"uuid":"ebd300ba-d73d-449c-aa44-8940c5170de8"}